virglrenderer: separable varying arrays end at location 31 - their last sid (#589)

What: a separable shader gives the generic varying of sid s the location 31 - s. On desktop
GL a generic varying array outside an interface block now ends there: it starts at 31 - its
last sid instead of 31 - its first. Between two separable stages (the key's separable_in and
separable_out, virgl-shader-separable-patch-varyings.patch) its elements are in reverse:
element i is sid last - i, at location 31 - (last - i), where the other stage has that sid as
a single varying or in its own reversed array. Every read and write of such an array reverses
its index (a dynamic index is clamped into the array), and so do the names transform feedback
records. Next to a whole stage the program is linked as one and matches by name: the elements
keep their order there. GLES and arrays in a block are unchanged.
Why: an array from 31 - its first sid ran past the last location ("Or active varying aliasing.
One or more slots starting at 31 unavailable for 'vso_g0' from layout location request"), so
Apple's GL refused every separable pipeline with a varying array, on the software renderer and
the M4 and M1 GPUs alike (macOS 27). Guest Mesa splits an array it reads with constant indices
into single varyings (constant IO offsets are folded), so only arrays read or written with a
dynamic index reach vrend as arrays. Arrays in a block (geometry and tessellation IO, vrend's
stand-in for arrays of arrays, which Apple's GL 4.1 lacks) take no location on GLSL 4.10 and
are still refused.
Upstream: not sent; virglrenderer main puts such arrays at 31 - their first sid (checked
2026-10-31).
Test: Tests/virgl/test-separable-pervertex.c on Apple's software renderer: an array read with
constant indices (green), an array into an array read with a dynamic index (E1), written with
one (E1b), an array into single varyings (E2) and the reverse (E3), interpolateAtCentroid and
interpolateAtSample on such an array, transform feedback of its second element (four
components and two); each was refused before. One program of a separable and a whole stage
(M3, M4): drawn right or refused, never drawn wrong. Arrays into or out of a geometry shader
(E5-E7) stay refused rows.

